        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Topical vitamin C has solid evidence for a few modest benefits, and several bigger promises that the research does not back.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Vitamin C serums have reasonable evidence for antioxidant protection, for limiting new UV-driven pigmentation and for smoother-looking sun-aged skin over months. The evidence is thin for instant brightening, extra hydration or quickly fading deep pigmentation. Judge a serum by a patient standard: steady daily use, under sunscreen, for several weeks to months.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Vitamin C serums carry a reputation that is bigger than their evidence. Some of the claims on the shelf are well supported, some come with conditions, and some are plain marketing.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Benefits the research supports
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Antioxidant protection.</strong> Reviews describe vitamin C as one of the most powerful antioxidants in the skin, protecting against photoaging and working together with vitamin E.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29104718/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Limiting new pigmentation from UV.</strong> A meta-analysis of 31 randomised trials in 741 healthy volunteers found that vitamin C reduced the pigmentation caused by simulated daylight in a dose-dependent way, with the strongest effect at 10%.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30881584/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Smoother sun-aged skin.</strong> In a double-blind trial lasting six months, a 5% vitamin C cream improved the look of photoaged skin and reduced deep furrows compared with a plain base.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12823436/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> A systematic review of seven trials in 139 volunteers found treated skin looked smoother and less wrinkled.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Each of these is a slow, supporting benefit. None of them is a transformation.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Benefits that come with conditions
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Fading dark spots.</strong> The pigmentation meta-analysis found that vitamin C worked as an antipigmenting agent, which means it limits new pigment, and that it added nothing once the depigmentation phase began.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30881584/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Another review did find objective lightening in treated skin, and it noted that long-term use may be needed to see noticeable change.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Supporting collagen.</strong> Skin naturally holds a lot of vitamin C, and it takes part in collagen synthesis.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28805671/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a> Clinical studies on topical formulations are still limited, though, and the challenge is finding a formula that is both stable and well absorbed.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29104718/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Helping melasma.</strong> The same systematic review described topical vitamin C as a possible alternative for melasma, while stating that more studies are needed to confirm the results and to find the ideal concentration.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a>
</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
