    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to judge a vitamin C serum
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A few checks cut through most of the noise on the shelf:
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Check the form.</strong> Derivatives of vitamin C are designed to be steadier than ascorbic acid, but the trials above do not prove the same result for a derivative.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Look at the timeline.</strong> A product that promises change in days is promising something the research does not show.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Look at the study details.</strong> A fair claim names the measure, the timepoint and the line that says results may vary.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Look at the routine.</strong> Plan on daily sunscreen and a moisturiser alongside the serum.</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        <a href="/products/deep-hydration-glow-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Deep Hydration, Glow Serum</a> contains a vitamin C derivative, 3-O-ethyl ascorbic acid, a stable form of vitamin C. It sits in a formula with niacinamide, five ceramides and cholesterol, and is used on damp skin, followed by moisturiser and morning sunscreen. Because the trials above did not test this derivative, read them as evidence for vitamin C in general. The serum's own clinical study is the evidence for this formula, and individual results may vary.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Treat a vitamin C serum as a long-term investment in how your skin ages, and not as a quick fix. Photograph your skin in the same light at the start, and then compare again after a few weeks and a few months.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>

    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does vitamin C brighten skin instantly?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. The trials that measured pigmentation and skin texture ran for days to months,<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30881584/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12823436/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a> and a fresher look in the first few minutes is a surface effect.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does vitamin C remove dark spots?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It works better at preventing new UV-driven pigment than at lightening what is already there.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30881584/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> Another review did find objective lightening, though it noted that noticeable change may need long-term use.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37128827/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is a higher vitamin C percentage better?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Not automatically. In the pigmentation meta-analysis, the strongest effect was at 10%,<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30881584/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> but reviews also point out that stability and absorption decide how much of the vitamin reaches the skin.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29104718/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does vitamin C help wrinkles?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">In a six-month trial on sun-aged skin, a 5% vitamin C cream reduced deep furrows and improved skin relief compared with a plain base.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12823436/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> That trial involved photoaged skin on the neck and arms, so results on the face may differ.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can vitamin C replace sunscreen?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Vitamin C is an antioxidant that supports skin dealing with sun exposure,<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29104718/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a> and sunscreen blocks UV before it reaches the skin. Use both.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is eating vitamin C enough for the skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Reviews say it is not well understood how topical vitamin C compares with dietary intake.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28805671/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a> Eating well supports skin from the inside, and a serum is an add-on and not a replacement.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
